# fork/verify/allowed-signers — who may sign an ore release tag.
#
# ssh-keygen's allowed_signers format: <principal> <keytype> <key>.
# Verify a tag with:
#   git -c gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile=fork/verify/allowed-signers verify-tag ore-vX.Y.Z
#
# Release tags are created by ore-sync-autoland from the `tagging` environment,
# which is gated to `main` and holds ORE_TAG_SIGNING_KEY and nothing else. The
# Apple Developer ID certificate and notarization key stay behind the separate
# `release` environment, which admits only `ore-v*` tags -- so nothing that can
# sign a tag can also reach the signing certificates, and vice versa.
#
# GitHub will show these tags as "Unverified" in the web UI: it verifies SSH
# signatures against keys registered to a GitHub ACCOUNT, and this key belongs
# to the repository's release automation rather than to a person. That is the
# honest provenance, and this file is what makes it checkable -- the property
# worth having is "the key this repository declares as its release signer made
# this tag", which is asserted here and verified in CI, not inferred from a badge.
release-automation@ore-cli ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAILb4Kas7TG/TSQNx/YwBzoQAVjUxReTePUo/q3oOYMKH
